WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:01.920
Instructor: Another variation of git diff

00:01.920 --> 00:05.310
is to pass in this option --staged

00:05.310 --> 00:07.260
or alternatively cached.

00:07.260 --> 00:09.570
They do the same thing, just an alias.

00:09.570 --> 00:13.200
Both of them will show us the staged changes,

00:13.200 --> 00:15.570
only the changes that are staged.

00:15.570 --> 00:17.550
So we've seen how to do all unstaged.

00:17.550 --> 00:20.910
We've seen how to do staged and unstaged,

00:20.910 --> 00:24.150
both of these options are how you do only stage changes.

00:24.150 --> 00:26.580
Only show me the staged changes.

00:26.580 --> 00:29.250
What is the difference between my last commit

00:29.250 --> 00:31.500
and the staging area?

00:31.500 --> 00:33.510
So I'll show this to you now.

00:33.510 --> 00:36.570
If we take a look at git status, the same project,

00:36.570 --> 00:40.020
project is a stretch, but the same repository,

00:40.020 --> 00:43.110
changes to be committed, numbers.txt.

00:43.110 --> 00:45.450
We added one into numbers,

00:45.450 --> 00:47.700
then we have some changes for the colors file,

00:47.700 --> 00:51.570
we added violet, but that is not part of the staging area.

00:51.570 --> 00:56.570
So right now, if I can type it, git diff -- and the staged,

00:58.890 --> 01:01.890
we only see those changes from numbers

01:01.890 --> 01:02.910
because that's the only...

01:02.910 --> 01:06.930
Change, versus if I do git diff on its own,

01:06.930 --> 01:10.020
this is unstaged changes so we see colors

01:10.020 --> 01:13.980
and if I do get diff head, we see both.

01:13.980 --> 01:15.390
That's everything that has changed.

01:15.390 --> 01:17.520
It doesn't care if it's staged or not

01:17.520 --> 01:21.960
and then if I add in colors, so if I do git add colors,

01:21.960 --> 01:25.590
now if I do git diff --staged, we see both

01:25.590 --> 01:27.150
because both of those changes

01:27.150 --> 01:28.770
have been staged at this point.

01:28.770 --> 01:30.483
Also, we can use cached.

01:31.560 --> 01:33.610
For whatever reason, I don't like cached.

01:34.740 --> 01:37.260
I call it the staging area, staged makes more sense to me.

01:37.260 --> 01:40.410
But I do believe the docs actually reference cached first

01:40.410 --> 01:42.900
and then say staged is an alias of cached.

01:42.900 --> 01:43.950
It doesn't matter.

01:43.950 --> 01:46.710
All right, so quick quiz here.

01:46.710 --> 01:48.120
Here's my git status.

01:48.120 --> 01:49.770
What will I see if I do git diff?

01:51.000 --> 01:52.140
Think about it.

01:52.140 --> 01:54.390
Okay, the answer is nothing.

01:54.390 --> 01:58.110
Well, I guess we see end, but there's no changes to display

01:58.110 --> 02:00.900
because git diff is showing us unstaged changes,

02:00.900 --> 02:02.670
there are no unstaged changes,

02:02.670 --> 02:04.833
everything has been staged at this point.
